    "result_text": "All 71 tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS), including the new `Cp59Tests` that asserts problem+json. Here's the situation:\n\n## Result: complete and green\n\nThe change is fully satisfied by the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, and the full suite (71 tests, 0 failures) passes.\n\n**How the spec is met.** Every 400 / 409 / 429 rejection in the app is raised as an exception that routes through `ExceptionControllerAdvice`:\n\n- **400** \u2014 `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (bean validation), `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` (missing/blank fields, bad postcode, bad telephone/email), `FutureRegistrationDateException`\n- **409** \u2014 `DuplicateOwnerTelephone/Email/Household/Identity` and `CityAtCapacityException`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`\n\nEach handler builds its body via `detailBuild(...)`, which returns a Spring `ProblemDetail`. That populates all four required RFC7807 members:\n\n- `type` \u2014 `problemDetail.setType(...)`\n- `title` \u2014 `problemDetail.setTitle(...)`\n- `status` \u2014 inherent to `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n- `detail` \u2014 `problemDetail.setDetail(...)`\n\nBecause the handlers return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, Spring MVC automatically serializes with `Content-Type: application/problem+json` and preserves the original HTTP status codes. I verified this end-to-end: `Cp59Tests.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` removes a required field, and the response comes back `400` + `application/problem+json` + `$.status == 400`.\n\nI confirmed there are no rejection paths that bypass the advice (all `throw new` sites in the REST layer map to the ProblemDetail handlers) and no test classes outside the acceptance package. No code changes were needed; the suite is green.",
